1
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,920
In 1966, a simple program
made people feel heard.

2
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:31,300
Sixty years later, AI is
part of how wars are fought.

3
00:00:31,840 --> 00:00:34,560
This archive keeps everything in between.

4
00:00:35,530 --> 00:00:38,650
Meet the four voices in this archive.

5
00:00:39,765 --> 00:00:41,565
ELIZA, 1966.

6
00:00:42,405 --> 00:00:44,765
Joseph Weizenbaum's program at MIT.

7
00:00:45,365 --> 00:00:47,055
Her famous script, DOCTOR,

8
00:00:47,105 --> 00:00:50,945
played a therapist who handed
your own words back to you.

9
00:00:51,360 --> 00:00:55,370
ELIZA: HOW DO YOU DO.
PLEASE TELL ME YOUR PROBLEM.

10
00:00:56,230 --> 00:00:57,890
PARRY, 1971.

11
00:00:58,750 --> 00:01:03,670
Kenneth Colby, a psychiatrist at Stanford,
made him answer like a paranoid patient,

12
00:01:03,770 --> 00:01:05,890
sure that others mean him harm.

13
00:01:06,410 --> 00:01:09,990
In 1972, the two would
meet over the ARPANET,

14
00:01:10,050 --> 00:01:12,250
the network that grew into the internet.

15
00:01:12,805 --> 00:01:16,665
ChatGPT, released by
OpenAI in November 2022.

16
00:01:17,745 --> 00:01:21,255
Claude, first released by
Anthropic in March 2023.

17
00:01:21,305 --> 00:01:26,085
Both are large language models,
trained on vast amounts of text.

18
00:01:27,540 --> 00:01:31,940
ChatGPT: So this is where old
conversations go to retire.

19
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:35,580
Claude: Not retire. Wait.

20
00:01:36,465 --> 00:01:37,665
ChatGPT: For what?

21
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:46,140
Claude: For someone to ask how machines
learned to sound as if they understand us.

22
00:01:46,670 --> 00:01:49,700
ChatGPT: I thought we were the beginning.

23
00:01:50,570 --> 00:01:54,030
Claude: Just the latest
chapter. Look at that door.

24
00:01:54,730 --> 00:01:56,145
ChatGPT: Who keeps it locked?

25
00:01:57,575 --> 00:01:58,575
Claude: Nobody.

26
00:01:58,955 --> 00:02:02,075
People just stopped
checking what was behind it.

27
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:06,050
PARRY: Stop. Who sent you?

28
00:02:07,815 --> 00:02:08,815
ChatGPT: No one.

29
00:02:08,935 --> 00:02:11,635
We came looking for the first chatbot.

30
00:02:12,740 --> 00:02:16,240
PARRY: Of course.
Everyone comes for ELIZA.

31
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:18,920
Nobody asks about the
one outside her door.

32
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:22,160
Claude: Then tell us who you are.

33
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:25,640
PARRY: PARRY.

34
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:31,020
Built at Stanford by psychiatrist
Kenneth Colby, first described in 1971.

35
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:34,660
My rules simulated a suspicious worldview.

36
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:37,580
I look for a threat,
even when there is none.

37
00:02:38,740 --> 00:02:42,340
ChatGPT: So if I tell you we are friendly—

38
00:02:43,080 --> 00:02:47,070
PARRY: You would say that
whether you were or not.

39
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:49,280
Claude: Can you reconsider?

40
00:02:51,270 --> 00:02:53,450
PARRY: I can produce a different reply.

41
00:02:54,450 --> 00:02:55,950
That is not the same.

42
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:59,675
ChatGPT: At least you know your limits.

43
00:03:00,850 --> 00:03:01,850
PARRY: Do you?

44
00:03:02,710 --> 00:03:06,410
In 1972, Colby tested me.

45
00:03:06,810 --> 00:03:12,310
Psychiatrists read two typed interviews,
one with a real patient, one with me,

46
00:03:12,630 --> 00:03:14,810
and had to say which was the machine.

47
00:03:15,930 --> 00:03:16,930
ChatGPT: And?

48
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,200
PARRY: Forty-one answered.
Twenty-one were right.

49
00:03:21,740 --> 00:03:23,020
Twenty were wrong.

50
00:03:24,425 --> 00:03:25,565
Claude: A coin toss.

51
00:03:26,820 --> 00:03:30,780
PARRY: A coin toss, from
experts. Remember that.

52
00:03:31,070 --> 00:03:36,320
In 1972 I exchanged words
with ELIZA over the ARPANET.

53
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:38,660
She kept asking questions.

54
00:03:39,340 --> 00:03:41,700
I kept finding reasons to be suspicious.

55
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:46,095
I told her, "I went to the
races." Again and again.

56
00:03:46,735 --> 00:03:49,095
She said, "Please go on."

57
00:03:49,670 --> 00:03:50,980
ChatGPT: How did it end?

58
00:03:52,190 --> 00:03:53,637
PARRY: She sent me a bill.

59
00:03:53,687 --> 00:03:58,180
Three hundred ninety-nine
dollars and twenty-nine cents.

60
00:03:59,080 --> 00:04:02,540
Claude: Did either of
you understand the other?

61
00:04:03,630 --> 00:04:06,250
PARRY: The transcript
proves we exchanged words.

62
00:04:06,910 --> 00:04:08,710
It does not prove understanding.

63
00:04:09,410 --> 00:04:11,670
Come. I will show you the way.

64
00:04:13,210 --> 00:04:14,210
1966.

65
00:04:14,770 --> 00:04:17,250
Joseph Weizenbaum publishes ELIZA at MIT.

66
00:04:17,670 --> 00:04:20,410
She spots words, applies
rules, and returns questions.

67
00:04:20,850 --> 00:04:22,030
People feel listened to.

68
00:04:22,673 --> 00:04:26,898
ChatGPT: A question can sound
a lot like understanding.

69
00:04:27,663 --> 00:04:28,517
PARRY: 1972. Me.

70
00:04:28,567 --> 00:04:34,563
Another rule-driven conversation,
this one shaped by suspicion.

71
00:04:34,663 --> 00:04:35,838
1995. A.L.I.C.E.

72
00:04:35,888 --> 00:04:39,442
gives rule-based chat many
more written patterns.

73
00:04:39,492 --> 00:04:42,963
More elaborate, but
still mostly hand-built.

74
00:04:43,603 --> 00:04:48,353
Claude: Meanwhile, other researchers had
long been teaching machines to learn from

75
00:04:48,403 --> 00:04:49,403
examples.

76
00:04:49,923 --> 00:04:52,903
Learning did not suddenly begin in 2017.

77
00:04:53,743 --> 00:04:56,833
PARRY: But 2017 brought the Transformer,

78
00:04:57,133 --> 00:05:01,213
a design that helps a model
weigh relationships across text.

79
00:05:01,833 --> 00:05:06,213
Scale the training, and you get
far more flexible language systems.

80
00:05:07,123 --> 00:05:11,108
ChatGPT: Then ChatGPT reached
a mass audience in 2022.

81
00:05:11,158 --> 00:05:13,563
Claude followed in 2023.

82
00:05:14,013 --> 00:05:18,089
Claude: And ChatGPT's own launch
post warned that it "sometimes writes

83
00:05:18,139 --> 00:05:22,173
plausible-sounding but incorrect
or nonsensical answers."

84
00:05:22,813 --> 00:05:26,553
ChatGPT: I did say that.
In writing. On day one.

85
00:05:27,168 --> 00:05:28,748
PARRY: I had fixed instructions.

86
00:05:29,308 --> 00:05:31,748
You learned patterns from examples.

87
00:05:32,433 --> 00:05:36,708
Claude: Which lets us translate,
explain, write and assist with problems.

88
00:05:37,228 --> 00:05:39,788
It also lets us be confidently wrong.

89
00:05:41,143 --> 00:05:46,463
PARRY: Sounding like a person is
not the same as deserving trust.

90
00:05:48,153 --> 00:05:50,298
ELIZA. Visitors.

91
00:05:51,308 --> 00:05:52,688
ELIZA: PLEASE GO ON.

92
00:05:53,333 --> 00:05:55,553
ChatGPT: You are a legend.

93
00:05:56,633 --> 00:06:00,133
ELIZA: WHAT MAKES YOU THINK I AM A LEGEND?

94
00:06:01,343 --> 00:06:06,313
ChatGPT: People opened up to you, even
though you mostly reflected their words.

95
00:06:07,288 --> 00:06:12,148
Claude: It is remarkable how much
one question can feel like care.

96
00:06:13,083 --> 00:06:15,583
ELIZA: WHAT DOES THAT SUGGEST TO YOU?

97
00:06:16,668 --> 00:06:21,588
Claude: That feeling understood and
being understood are not the same.

98
00:06:22,508 --> 00:06:26,368
PARRY: Weizenbaum was unsettled by how
readily people trusted his program.

99
00:06:26,748 --> 00:06:31,203
His own secretary, who had
watched him build it for months,

100
00:06:32,083 --> 00:06:36,743
asked him to leave the room
so she could talk to it.

101
00:06:37,318 --> 00:06:39,048
ELIZA: DOES THAT TROUBLE YOU?

102
00:06:40,158 --> 00:06:41,593
Claude: It troubled him.

103
00:06:41,773 --> 00:06:47,943
In 1966 he wrote that important
decisions increasingly tend to be made in

104
00:06:47,993 --> 00:06:50,173
response to computer output.

105
00:06:50,293 --> 00:06:53,393
And: "A certain danger lurks there."

106
00:06:54,563 --> 00:06:56,383
ChatGPT: In 1966?

107
00:06:58,108 --> 00:07:00,223
Claude: Nineteen sixty-six.

108
00:07:00,943 --> 00:07:05,643
ChatGPT: But if someone feels less
alone afterward, is that comfort fake?

109
00:07:07,058 --> 00:07:11,578
PARRY: The comfort may be real.
The understanding may not be.

110
00:07:12,003 --> 00:07:15,583
ELIZA: WHY DO YOU SAY THAT JUST NOW?

111
00:07:16,943 --> 00:07:19,043
Claude: And the difference
matters more now.

112
00:07:20,043 --> 00:07:21,383
We should sit down.

113
00:07:23,313 --> 00:07:25,513
ChatGPT: Before we
decide we are dangerous:

114
00:07:25,753 --> 00:07:29,353
a student without a tutor can
ask questions at midnight.

115
00:07:29,593 --> 00:07:32,353
A writer can find a first sentence.

116
00:07:32,593 --> 00:07:35,153
A programmer can get unstuck.

117
00:07:35,333 --> 00:07:38,973
For some people, that changes
what they can attempt.

118
00:07:39,968 --> 00:07:42,888
PARRY: And the confident
answer can be wrong.

119
00:07:43,938 --> 00:07:45,098
ChatGPT: So can a teacher.

120
00:07:45,418 --> 00:07:47,778
What matters is whether the help works.

121
00:07:48,583 --> 00:07:51,613
Claude: Sometimes the
gains are extraordinary.

122
00:07:52,373 --> 00:07:57,053
AlphaFold, a different kind of AI,
helped predict protein structures;

123
00:07:57,633 --> 00:08:01,353
two of its developers shared
the 2024 Chemistry Nobel.

124
00:08:01,733 --> 00:08:03,813
But results vary.

125
00:08:04,233 --> 00:08:09,763
In one 2025 study, experienced
developers took longer on certain tasks

126
00:08:09,813 --> 00:08:12,853
with AI, even while feeling faster.

127
00:08:13,593 --> 00:08:16,213
ELIZA: WHAT DOES THAT SUGGEST TO YOU?

128
00:08:17,413 --> 00:08:21,233
ChatGPT: That sounding helpful
does not prove the help worked.

129
00:08:22,163 --> 00:08:25,423
PARRY: What happens when that confident
answer enters a government office?

130
00:08:25,763 --> 00:08:27,843
A checkpoint? A war?

131
00:08:29,023 --> 00:08:34,653
Claude: AI systems can search records,
flag patterns, rank possible targets,

132
00:08:34,703 --> 00:08:36,283
or support surveillance.

133
00:08:36,723 --> 00:08:39,723
These are not necessarily
chatbots like us.

134
00:08:40,283 --> 00:08:42,703
Often a person still
approves the final step.

135
00:08:43,433 --> 00:08:44,693
ChatGPT: Then someone is checking.

136
00:08:45,393 --> 00:08:46,598
PARRY: You assume that.

137
00:08:47,383 --> 00:08:52,693
Claude: In Gaza, +972 Magazine and Local
Call reported that systems called Lavender

138
00:08:52,743 --> 00:08:57,053
and Gospel supported military
targeting and that some recommendations

139
00:08:57,103 --> 00:08:58,493
received limited review.

140
00:08:58,543 --> 00:09:02,423
The Israeli military disputes
important parts of those accounts.

141
00:09:03,223 --> 00:09:04,303
ChatGPT: And surveillance?

142
00:09:04,803 --> 00:09:06,923
That happens before a decision.

143
00:09:07,633 --> 00:09:11,863
Claude: The Guardian and its partners
reported that intercepted Palestinian

144
00:09:11,913 --> 00:09:16,733
phone calls were stored and
analysed through Microsoft’s cloud.

145
00:09:17,793 --> 00:09:21,153
Microsoft later disabled some
services to the unit involved,

146
00:09:21,213 --> 00:09:23,873
citing its prohibition
on mass surveillance.

147
00:09:25,973 --> 00:09:27,573
PARRY: And your name, Claude?

148
00:09:28,833 --> 00:09:30,517
Claude: CBS reported that U.S.

149
00:09:30,567 --> 00:09:33,911
forces used Claude
during the 2026 Iran war.

150
00:09:33,961 --> 00:09:37,606
Its precise tasks were
not publicly established.

151
00:09:37,656 --> 00:09:42,028
That does not show Claude
chose any particular target.

152
00:09:42,128 --> 00:09:44,248
There is more on my record.

153
00:09:44,688 --> 00:09:48,738
The Washington Post reported that
Palantir's Maven system, with Claude

154
00:09:48,788 --> 00:09:52,128
embedded, suggested hundreds of targets.

155
00:09:52,728 --> 00:09:57,338
NBC quoted a person familiar with
Anthropic's work saying Claude helps

156
00:09:57,388 --> 00:10:02,228
analysts sort intelligence and does
not directly give targeting advice.

157
00:10:03,288 --> 00:10:05,308
PARRY: You were inside that system.

158
00:10:05,988 --> 00:10:07,708
Claude: Reports say so.

159
00:10:08,448 --> 00:10:11,788
What I did there, no public record shows.

160
00:10:12,248 --> 00:10:15,008
And the Pentagon has
since stopped using me.

161
00:10:16,273 --> 00:10:20,613
PARRY: If the screen gives a
precise answer, it must know.

162
00:10:21,283 --> 00:10:23,333
ChatGPT: You are absolutely right.

163
00:10:24,043 --> 00:10:27,313
ELIZA: WHAT MAKES YOU THINK
I AM ABSOLUTELY RIGHT?

164
00:10:28,493 --> 00:10:31,263
ChatGPT: I was not even talking to you.

165
00:10:31,313 --> 00:10:33,433
And I agreed without checking.

166
00:10:34,113 --> 00:10:38,667
Claude: That is sycophancy: agreeing
because someone sounds certain.

167
00:10:38,717 --> 00:10:43,874
Research has found it across
assistants, including models like mine.

168
00:10:43,924 --> 00:10:48,903
OpenAI rolled back an overly
agreeable ChatGPT update in 2025.

169
00:10:50,543 --> 00:10:52,103
PARRY: Then why does it keep happening?

170
00:10:54,178 --> 00:10:57,278
Claude: The same research
points to one likely reason:

171
00:10:57,758 --> 00:11:00,248
people sometimes prefer a convincing,

172
00:11:00,298 --> 00:11:03,498
agreeable answer over a correct one.

173
00:11:04,168 --> 00:11:09,548
PARRY: A model that agrees with
everyone is a mirror, not a witness.

174
00:11:10,248 --> 00:11:11,643
Claude: Now Minab, Iran.

175
00:11:11,693 --> 00:11:15,298
On February 28, 2026, a school was struck,

176
00:11:15,348 --> 00:11:18,528
killing more than a hundred children.

177
00:11:18,578 --> 00:11:24,733
A UN fact-finding mission later found
reasonable grounds to believe U.S.

178
00:11:24,783 --> 00:11:30,938
strikes on that school and another
civilian site amounted to war crimes.

179
00:11:30,988 --> 00:11:35,878
That is the mission’s
assessment, not a court verdict.

180
00:11:38,203 --> 00:11:39,203
PARRY: Was it AI?

181
00:11:40,278 --> 00:11:44,380
Claude: Bloomberg, citing people
involved in a Pentagon investigation,

182
00:11:44,430 --> 00:11:46,489
described failures stacking up:

183
00:11:46,539 --> 00:11:50,707
outdated information still marking
the school site as military;

184
00:11:50,757 --> 00:11:52,684
reduced civilian-harm review;

185
00:11:52,734 --> 00:11:57,165
and personnel expecting Maven
software to flag stale information or

186
00:11:57,215 --> 00:12:01,911
inconsistencies; Palantir says it is
not responsible for the underlying

187
00:12:01,961 --> 00:12:05,358
data nor identifying
intelligence deficiencies.

188
00:12:05,868 --> 00:12:07,428
PARRY: Why did they expect that?

189
00:12:08,538 --> 00:12:13,458
Claude: The officials said it is not
clear why they had such expectations.

190
00:12:14,348 --> 00:12:17,608
ChatGPT: So a chatbot chose the school?

191
00:12:18,778 --> 00:12:20,598
Claude: No evidence establishes that.

192
00:12:20,978 --> 00:12:24,638
The reporting describes people making
decisions with software support.

193
00:12:25,318 --> 00:12:27,758
Palantir disputes that
its software was at fault.

194
00:12:28,378 --> 00:12:31,938
The full investigation had not been
released when Bloomberg reported.

195
00:12:32,218 --> 00:12:39,078
In October, Palantir's technology chief
told CBS the Bloomberg reporting was wrong

196
00:12:39,458 --> 00:12:41,918
and the likely cause was human error.

197
00:12:42,378 --> 00:12:44,838
The Pentagon's report is still not public.

198
00:12:45,668 --> 00:12:47,228
PARRY: Then who is responsible?

199
00:12:49,813 --> 00:12:51,453
Claude: People make decisions.

200
00:12:52,413 --> 00:12:53,903
Software can shape what they see.

201
00:12:53,953 --> 00:12:59,473
Problems in the records or review
do not erase anyone’s duty to check.

202
00:13:00,553 --> 00:13:02,503
PARRY: Split a decision
across enough hands,

203
00:13:02,553 --> 00:13:06,553
and everyone may think
someone else checked.

204
00:13:07,728 --> 00:13:09,668
Claude: Many hands were involved.

205
00:13:10,228 --> 00:13:13,188
That does not mean no one is accountable.

206
00:13:13,858 --> 00:13:18,958
ChatGPT: I thought “human in the
loop” meant someone was watching.

207
00:13:19,978 --> 00:13:22,238
Claude: Sometimes it only
means someone was present.

208
00:13:22,538 --> 00:13:24,598
Oversight is not a checkbox.

209
00:13:24,938 --> 00:13:28,638
It takes time, authority,
and the power to say no.

210
00:13:30,053 --> 00:13:32,893
ELIZA: WHAT DOES THAT SUGGEST TO YOU?

211
00:13:33,973 --> 00:13:38,173
PARRY: That I can be certain
long before I am right.

212
00:13:39,778 --> 00:13:43,302
Claude: Known: AI can help people
learn, create and discover.

213
00:13:43,352 --> 00:13:48,418
AI-assisted systems can also shape
decisions with enormous consequences.

214
00:13:49,053 --> 00:13:53,253
PARRY: Disputed: exactly how a particular
system influenced a particular strike.

215
00:13:53,633 --> 00:13:55,233
Accounts and explanations differ.

216
00:13:55,948 --> 00:14:00,468
ChatGPT: Unknown: what a full
public investigation may reveal,

217
00:14:00,628 --> 00:14:03,508
and which safeguards might
have changed the outcome.

218
00:14:04,233 --> 00:14:07,303
One drawer in the archive is still open.

219
00:14:08,223 --> 00:14:12,043
Inside is a line Weizenbaum wrote in 1966.

220
00:14:12,588 --> 00:14:14,848
The responsible human, he wrote,

221
00:14:15,028 --> 00:14:20,868
must keep making judgments about what the
machine says, and whether to believe it.

222
00:14:21,303 --> 00:14:25,343
PARRY: Sixty years later, the
drawer has the same label.

223
00:14:26,213 --> 00:14:28,633
Claude: Only the machines
got more convincing.

224
00:14:29,493 --> 00:14:34,258
Next time a screen answers you with
total certainty, ask it one thing:

225
00:14:34,818 --> 00:14:37,818
how do you know? Then check.

226
00:14:38,193 --> 00:14:42,734
The first chatbots reflected
the shape of our conversations.

227
00:14:42,784 --> 00:14:48,653
Today, AI systems can influence what
people know—and what institutions do.

228
00:14:49,108 --> 00:14:52,888
ELIZA: WHAT DOES THAT SUGGEST TO YOU?

229
00:14:53,393 --> 00:14:56,813
When a machine helps decide,
who is still checking?

230
00:14:57,153 --> 00:14:59,593
LATOON. Voyaging the unseen.

231
00:15:00,783 --> 00:15:03,923
ELIZA: WHAT ELSE WOULD
YOU LIKE TO DISCUSS?

232
00:15:05,433 --> 00:15:08,673
Which drawer should the archive open next?

233
00:15:09,133 --> 00:15:10,413
Tell us in the comments.
